7 T8 T9 chow.  \n\nThus from hand T7 T7 T8 T9 T9 J1 J1 B8:  \nIf we discard B8 now, we keep T7 T7 T8 T9 T9 J1 J1.  \nWe need two melds + pair. Pair could be J1 J1.  \nMelds: J1 pung (need one more J1), T7 pung (need one more T7), T9 pung (need one more T9). But we have T8 lone.  \n\nSo discard T8 instead?  \n\nLet\u2019s compare discards:  \n\n**Discard B8**: keeps bamboo + honors. Tiles left: T7 T7 T8 T9 T9 J1 J1.  \nWe can win if we draw:  \n- J1 \u2192 J1 pung, pair from T7 or T9, and need one more meld from bamboo (T7 pung or T9 pung or T8 chow with T7 T9? not possible). Actually, if we draw J1, hand: T7 T7 T8 T9 T9 J1 J1 J1.  \nGroup: J1 pung (meld), J1 pair gone, so need pair: could be T7 T7 or T9 T9. Remaining: T8 T9 or T8 T7 \u2192 not a meld. So not complete. So discard B8 might not be good.  \n\n**Discard T8**: hand: T7 T7 T9 T9 B8 J1 J1.  \nNow we have two pairs (T7, T9) and B8 single, J1 pair. Wait for:  \n- J1 \u2192 J1 pung, pair T7 or T9, B8 lone \u2192 not complete.  \n- T7 \u2192 T7 pung, pair J1 or T9, B8 lone \u2192 not complete.  \n- T9 \u2192 similar.  \nSo discard T8 leaves us needing two melds still, but we only have enough tiles for one meld (if we get a third of one number) and a pair.  \n\nActually, we must think: we have 8 tiles in hand, need 4 melds + 1 pair total in winning hand. We already have 2 melds (J2 pung, B4 kong). So from 8 hand tiles, we must form exactly 2 melds + 1 pair. That\u2019s 2\u00d73 + 2 = 8 tiles. Perfect.  \n\nSo grouping:  \nPossible groups from T7 T7 T8 T9 T9 B8 J1 J1:  \nOption A: J1 J1 (pair), T7 T8 T9 (chow), T7 T9 B8 \u2192 not a meld. Fail.  \nOption B: J1 J1 J1 (meld), T7 T7 (pair), T8 T9 B8 \u2192 not a meld. Fail.  \nOption C: T7 T7 T7 (meld), T9 T9 (pair), J1 J1 (pair) \u2192 too many pairs. Fail.  \n\nWe must use all 8 tiles in 2 melds + 1 pair.  \n\nTry:  \n1. J1 J1 (pair)  \n2. T7 T8 T9 (chow)  \n3. T7 T9 B8 \u2192 not valid.  \n\nTry:  \n1. J1 J1 J1 (meld)  \n2. T7 T7 (pair)  \n3. T8 T9 B8 \u2192 invalid.  \n\nSo current hand cannot make 2 melds + 1 pair with any draw unless we break B8 or T8 into something.  \n\nIf we discard B8: hand T7 T7 T8 T9 T9 J1 J1 (7 tiles? Wait, that\u2019s 7 tiles, but we need 8 tiles in hand. Oops, miscount: discard B8 \u2192 remove 1 tile \u2192 7 tiles left? No, we start with 8 tiles, discard 1 \u2192 7 left, but we need 8 to complete 2 melds + pair (8 tiles). So we must have 8 tiles before drawing winning tile. So we\u2019re just choosing which tile to discard now to improve shape.  \n\nWe need to be in tenpai after this discard (waiting for 1 tile to complete 2 melds + pair).  \n\nCheck if we can be in tenpai now:  \n\nGroup as:  \n- (meld) T7 T8 T9 chow  \n- (meld) J1 J1 J1 pung (need one J1)  \n- (pair) T7 T9 \u2192 not a pair.  \n\nSo no.  \n\nWhat if we group:  \n- (meld) T7 T7 T7 pung (need one T7)  \n- (meld) T9 T9 T9 pung (need one T9)  \n- (pair) J1 J1  \nThen T8 and B8 are extra \u2192 discard T8 and B8 in two turns? But we can only discard one now.  \n\nSo we must discard one of T8 or B8 to move toward tenpai.  \n\nB8 is completely alone, no connection to other suits in hand. T8 connects to T7 and T9 for chow possibilities. So discard B8 seems better to keep bamboo integrity.  \n\nAlso, unseen tiles: B8 has 3 left (total 4, 1 in hand, 0 in melds? Wait, unshown: B8:3 means 3 left in wall/dead? So B8 is relatively safe to discard? Not necessarily for defense, but we\u2019re building hand.  \n\nBut more importantly, J1: unshown J1:3 (total 4, we have 2, so 1 left). So J1 is very scarce.  \n\nT7: unshown T7:3 (total 4, we have 2, so 1 left).  \nT9: unshown T9:3 (total 4, we have 2, so 1 left).  \n\nSo actually, waiting for T7 or T9 pung is possible (1 left each). Waiting for J1 pung (1 left).  \n\nBest shape:  \nKeep T7 T7 T9 T9 J1 J1, discard T8 and B8 over two turns.  \n\nBut now we must discard one: B8 or T8?  \n\nIf we discard B8 now, we keep T7 T7 T8 T9 T9 J1 J1. Then next turn if we draw well, we can discard T8 if needed.  \n\nIf we discard T8 now, we keep T7 T7 T9 T9 B8 J1 J1, then need to discard B8 next.  \n\nBoth similar, but T8 can be used in chow if we draw T6 or T9/T7, but we already have T7 T9 pairs, so chow not useful if we want All Pungs.  \n\nFor All Pungs, we want T7 pung and T9 pung and J1 pung, plus J2 pung and B4 kong already. That\u2019s 5 melds? Too many. Wait, we only need 4 melds + pair. We have J2 pung and B4 kong already (2 melds). We need 2 more melds from hand. If we get T7 pung and J1 pung, that\u2019s 4 melds total, and pair from T9 T9. That works:  \nMelds: J2, B4, T7, J1 \u2192 all pungs/kongs. Pair: T9 T9.  \n\nSo we need: T7 (1 more), J1 (1 more), and keep T9 T9 as pair. So hand must be: T7 T7 T7, J1 J1 J1, T9 T9, plus extra T8 and B8 now. So discard T8 and B8.  \n\nSo discard B8 now is fine.  \n\n**Safety check**: B8 is relatively safe? Other players\u2019 melds: next player has B7 B8 B9 chow, so B8 is in his chow, less likely he needs another. Across player has none. Previous player has B1 B2 B3 chow. So B8 is fairly safe.  \n\nT8: across player has T7 T8 T9 chow already, so T8 is also safe.  \n\nSo both safe. Choose B8 to keep bamboo shape.  \n\n---\n\n**Evaluation**: Discarding B8 keeps bamboo suit intact to possibly form T7/T9 pungs or chow, moves toward All Pungs + Two Dragon Pungs for high score (12+ points).  \n\n**Reason**: B8 is isolated, no link to other suits in hand; keeping T8 preserves chow option if needed, but we aim for pung-based hand for All Pungs. Still, discarding B8 first is safe and progresses hand toward tenpai with waits on T7 or J1.  \n\n**Answer**: Play B8  \n\n---\n\n**Final answer**:  \n\nEvaluation: Discard B8 to maintain bamboo suit cohesion and move toward All Pungs + Two Dragon Pungs scoring combination.  \nReason: B8 is isolated, safe given opponents\u2019 melds, and keeping T8 allows potential chow if draw changes plan, but primary aim is pung-based hand for higher points.  \nAnswer: Play B8"}
