n count is 3 (initial 4, previous player played F1 once). So 1 seen, 3 left.  \n\nBut I need to think about winning potential. My hand currently has no clear path to high points. I have two dragon pungs possible if I get another J1 (J1 pung would give another dragon pung, 2 points) and I already have J2 pung (2 points). That's 4 points from dragons alone. Plus maybe all simples (no terminals/honors except I have F1 and J1, so not all simples). I have terminals/honors, so not all simples.  \n\nI also have two pairs, so I could go for seven pairs? But I have two melds already exposed, so seven pairs is impossible because seven pairs requires a concealed hand (no exposed melds). So I must complete a standard 4 melds + pair hand.  \n\nBest plan: try to complete a pung of J1 (since I have two J1 already) or a chow using W7 (like W5,W6,W7 or W7,W8,W9). But W8 is dead (0 left), so W7,W8,W9 impossible. W5,W6,W7 possible: W5 unseen 4, W6 unseen 4, W7 unseen 1. So low probability.  \n\nAlternatively, I could discard one of my pairs to try to get a more coherent hand. But discarding a pair reduces my pair count. Currently I have two pairs, which is good because I need one pair for final hand. If I discard one of T7 or T9, I lose a pair and have to form a new pair from singles, which is harder.  \n\nSo I should keep both pairs for now and discard a single. My singles are W7, F1, and one of the J1? Wait, I have two J1, that's a pair, not single. Actually, singles: W7 (1 tile), F1 (1 tile), and then J1 pair (2 tiles). So I have three singles: W7, F1, and the J1 pair is actually a pair, not single. So I could discard W7 or F1.  \n\nDiscarding F1: F1 is East wind, not my seat wind or prevailing wind, so no value for me. But it's an honor tile, if I keep it, I could get a pung if I draw two more, but unlikely (3 left). Discarding W7: W7 is a simple tile, could be used in chow, but with W8 dead, chow options limited.  \n\nLooking at opponents' discards:  \n- Next player has played J1