ble. It has only one copy left in the wall, reducing the risk of feeding an opponent's pung or chow. Discarding it preserves my pairs (T7 and T9) and keeps options open for drawing into a J1 pung or other combinations. Defensively, it is safer than discarding honors (F1, J1) which are more likely to be called for pungs, or discarding T7/T9 which are live and could complete opponents' sequences.  \n\nAnswer: Play W7"}
